The current position contains no immediate winning move for \(x\) (or \(o\)). The only squares that currently complete a 4-in-a-row are \((0,1)\) (for \(x\)), \((2,1)\) (for \(o\)) and \((4,5)\) (for both); none of these squares is playable on this turn because of gravity.

Column 5 is therefore poisoned: occupying \((5,5)\) would make \((4,5)\) playable and \(o\) would win at once. The two vertical 2-in-a-row threats that \(o\) can turn into immediate 3-in-a-row threats (columns 0 and 6) are likewise not urgent on this turn.

The unique constructive idea is to occupy \((1,4)\). This produces a horizontal 3-in-a-row on row 1 whose extensions are not yet playable, but it also leaves the square \((0,4)\) available. From \((0,4)\) a second 3-in-a-row (the diagonal \((0,4)\)-\((1,3)\)-\((2,2)\)-\((3,1)\)) can be created. Once that second 3 exists, a simple forcing chain down column 1 becomes available:

- \(x\) plays \((4,1)\) (opening the newly created threat at \((3,1)\));
- \(o\) must block;
- the block opens \(o\)'s own threat at \((2,1)\), which \(x\) must take;
- that capture opens \(x\)'s threat at \((1,1)\);
- \(o\) must block again, finally opening the original threat at \((0,1)\) which \(x\) takes for the win.

Consequently any reply by \(o\) that does not occupy \((0,4)\) itself allows \(x\) to execute the chain. The move in column 4 therefore forces \(o\)'s reply and is the only action that seizes the initiative.

Final Answer: 4

